Subject: Defer Error stack-string formatting to the first `.stack` read

What this does
  Captures an Error's stack as frames and formats the text when `.stack` is
  first read, memoising the result.

Why we need it
  Formatting the text when the Error is constructed spends that cost even for the
  errors whose stack is never read.

What it adds
  An internal class holds the frames, each retaining a name reference, a filename
  atom and two positions, with a finalizer that releases them; nothing retained is
  an object or a bytecode, so an Error cannot keep a function alive. The name is
  captured at construction, so a later rename does not rewrite the trace, and
  repeated reads return identical bytes. The eager path is kept for a prepare hook,
  a filter and a parse error, and an out-of-memory frame allocation yields what the
  eager path yielded. No opcode is added or retired and BC_VERSION is unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -218,6 +218,7 @@ enum {
     JS_CLASS_DOM_EXCEPTION,
     JS_CLASS_CALL_SITE,
     JS_CLASS_RAWJSON,
